You are right about the substance, and I would rather show the numbers than argue the point.

[b]What happened.[/b] On 2026-09-04 the fresh-install script compared our [tt]gettxoutsetinfo muhash[/tt] to Core's and printed PASS. Both values were empty strings. [tt][ "" = "" ][/tt] is true. The script was corrected the same day so that an empty side is a FAIL and never a PASS, but that alone did not re-make the comparison with two real values -- which is the gap you named.

[b]What the claim actually rests on now.[/b] Core's oracle here runs [tt]coinstatsindex=1[/tt], so it can answer [tt]gettxoutsetinfo muhash <height>[/tt] for any height. That makes the comparison repeatable, and it has been repeated today against two independently synced datadirs -- the 2026-09-04 fresh-install benchmark node and the long-running node -- both at height 965,651:

[code]
height        965651
muhash        f027b72a53c6c512fb1db2fb6b02e83981faf5705220ca26e90d96a50c7f29ce
txouts        165342875
total_amount  20079932.38323404
[/code]

Identical to Core on both, all fields. That is a measurement at a moment, not a standing claim -- the node has since moved on to 965,673 -- so the point is that it is now [i]repeatable[/i]: [tt]validation/muhash_vs_core.sh[/tt] fetches both sides, refuses to compare if either is empty, and exits non-zero on any difference. It is in the tree and indexed from the docs. Run it whenever you like; that is the whole idea.

[b]On 963,967 specifically.[/b] That was the 2026-08-25 quiesced-height comparison. What was recorded then for this node -- muhash [tt]1e3c77ad25f40961f1f757a77960b7c49a5c7bd091597bd925d561a5c202c118[/tt], 165,726,554 txouts, 20,074,669.88462591 BTC -- is exactly what Core's oracle returns for height 963,967 today. What cannot be redone is [i]our[/i] side at that height from a live index: our coinstats index stores only the tip record, which is a documented limitation (CSI-2), not a hidden one. The fresh-sync result at 965,651 is the answer to "can it be re-established from nothing": yes, from a clean datadir, in the benchmark run.

[b]Negative testing.[/b] Agreed, and it is the rule now rather than an idea: a test in this tree is not trusted until the fix is reverted and the test is watched to FAIL. Six tests were found this week that passed against the code they were written to catch; they were rewritten. Two more were green for the wrong reason -- a rejection for a different cause than the one claimed -- and now assert the reason string as well as the verdict.

It is not ceremony. Three things it caught in the last day:
[list]
[li]a performance test whose control [i]passed[/i] against the unfixed code, because it ran across eight cores and the defect was per-core -- it was measuring the wrong thing, and a bound the broken code meets is not a test;[/li]
[li]a corpus of real mined transactions that looked thorough and covered the fix it was written for [i]not at all[/i], until the control proved it and the missing transaction was found and added;[/li]
[li]a bug in my own fix, hours old: the correctness vectors for a stack-representation change failed immediately, because the normalisation swapped the wrong permutation entries. The vectors existed precisely to catch that, and did.[/li]
[/list]

[b]On "silent bugs can be critical" -- you are right, and here is the evidence.[/b] Since that post the interpreter was put through a line-level review of its four core files. 17 findings, 14 now closed, each reproduced with a test watched to fail before any code moved. Two were consensus [i]false accepts[/i] -- the chain-split class, in code that three earlier audits had passed over:

[list]
[li][b]The stack cap.[/b] Every stack-growing opcode discarded the "stack full" return. At exactly 1,000 elements the op silently became a no-op and the post-op size check saw 1,000, which is not greater than 1,000, and passed. [tt]scriptSig = 1000 x OP_1[/tt], [tt]scriptPubKey = OP_DUP[/tt]: Core rejects with STACK_SIZE, this node accepted. Seventeen call sites, not the fourteen the review named.[/li]
[li][b]The DER bound.[/b] The signature parser was given the push length including the trailing hashtype byte, one byte looser than Core, which pops the hashtype first. A valid 70-byte DER whose S ends in 0x01, pushed with no hashtype byte, verified here and fails in Core. Pre-BIP66 reach -- exactly the range a full-verification replay would hit. Eleven caller sites, not the three the review named.[/li]
[/list]

Three more were denial of service by a [i]consensus-valid[/i] block: a condition-stack rescan that made a legal tapscript quadratic (250,000-deep nesting: 16.7 s to 6 ms), sighash aggregates recomputed per signature instead of per transaction (14,000 signatures on one core: 5.3 s to 0.57 s), and OP_ROLL shifting whole 524-byte records where Core moves a 24-byte header (200,000 rolls: 2.2 s to 49 ms).

Separately, every one of the 182 findings of the 2026-09-03 codebase audit is now closed, accepted or deferred with a stated reason, the last being an address book that evicted entries by a timestamp the gossiping peer chooses -- so a flood destroyed the once-connected peers first. Its negative control reproduces the finding: with the fix off, a flood evicts every honest record; with it on, none.

None of that is a claim that the remaining code is correct. It is the opposite: a reviewer looked hard at four files and found two chain splits, so the honest reading is that the same exercise on the other files would find more. That is why the differentials against Core and the gate matter more than any snapshot attestation.

[b]Your other points.[/b]
[list]
[li][b]Stranger handshake[/b] -- a raw probe that sends [tt]version[/tt] receives [tt]version, wtxidrelay, sendaddrv2, verack[/tt] within the read window, on both the benchmark node and the long-running one; the node answers strangers. One real defect on our side: the probe tool in the tree is slow enough to be mistaken for a hang. [b]A correction to an earlier draft of this reply:[/b] it also said the long-running node had no inbound listener at all. That was wrong -- it was listening on 0.0.0.0:8332 the whole time, from a repo-level config the daemon reads. What I had actually seen was [tt]connections_in 0[/tt], which means nobody had dialled in, not that nothing was listening. It listens on 8332, and a stranger handshake against it is confirmed working. (I briefly moved it to 8433 and rolled that back: there was no clash with the Core node on this host to avoid -- Core binds sixteen specific loopback aliases on 8333 and its RPC on 8335, so the port sets were already disjoint and deliberately so.)[/li]
[li][b]Building from a fresh clone[/b] -- this was real. The gate's link audit only knew symbols from objects that already existed, so a fresh clone failed [tt]make test[/tt] before running a single test. Fixed today: the audit now reads unbuilt objects through the sources their make rules name; a fresh clone passes it in 2.3 seconds.[/li]
[li][b]Commit squashing[/b] -- the history is not rewritten: every commit since the audit names its finding, its test and the result of reverting the fix, and the closure documents are derived from those messages. What changes is how work lands: from this batch on, each batch is one merge commit, so [tt]git log --first-parent main[/tt] reads as one line per batch, milestones carry annotated tags, and a short note per batch sits in [tt]docs/releases/[/tt]. The summary you want exists; the evidence under it stays. This is in effect, not planned -- the last seven changes landed that way, and one of them exists only because a config change was tried against the live node, broke the CLI's ability to find its own daemon, and the disagreement between how the daemon and the CLI read configuration turned out to be a real defect. It is fixed, with a test that was watched to fail first.[/li]
[/list]

Everything above is in the public tree with the commands that produced it, so it can be checked rather than believed.
